{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "nssp_companion_guide#1",
      "doc_id": "nssp_companion_guide",
      "heading": "Companion Guide: NSSP Emergency Department Data on Respiratory Illness > Introduction",
      "text": "The National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP) is a collaboration among CDC, local and state health departments, with private sector partners. Together, these partners collect, share, and analyze automated electronic healthcare data in near-real time.\n\nThe national data include emergency departments in all 50 states, the District of Columbia, and Guam. View the coverage map here."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "nssp_about#2",
      "doc_id": "nssp_about",
      "heading": "About NSSP > Syndromic surveillance",
      "text": "Syndromic surveillance provides public health officials with a timely system for detecting, understanding, and monitoring health threats. By tracking symptoms of patients in EDs and other settings\u2014before and after a diagnosis is confirmed, all in near real-time\u2014public health can detect unusual levels of illness to determine whether a response is needed. Public health also uses syndromic data to monitor disease trends.\n\nSyndromic data can serve as an early warning system to protect Americans from respiratory viruses, environmental threats, avoidable injuries, emerging diseases, and more.\n\nSyndromic surveillance is a collaborative effort among local and state health departments, CDC, and partners."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_sources#4",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_sources",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data sources > 2. Emergency department visits: NSSP",
      "text": "- **Dataset:** NSSP Emergency Department Visit Trajectories by State and Sub State Regions - COVID-19, Flu, RSV, Combined.\n- **Publisher:** CDC, National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP).\n- **Link:** [rdmq-nq56](https://data.cdc.gov/Public-Health-Surveillance/NSSP-Emergency-Department-Visit-Trajectories-by-St/rdmq-nq56)\n- **Releases used:** <!-- auto:releases_nssp -->54<!-- /auto:releases_nssp --> dated downloads of the full file, the latest dated <!-- auto:newest_nssp -->2026-10-14<!-- /auto:newest_nssp -->.\n- **Fields used:** percent of ED visits (raw and smoothed) for COVID-19, influenza, RSV, and all three combined, plus CDC's trend label for each pathogen."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#13",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Emergency department visit trends",
      "text": "Summary:\n\n- Describes changes in the percentage of emergency department visits for COVID-19, flu, or RSV.\n\nExplanation:\n\n- Collected through the National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP). The trends show changes in the percent of all visits to the emergency department that are for COVID-19, flu, and RSV.\n- CDC models the rate of change and shares that it is increasing or decreasing if confident in the change based on the model. If there is no clear trend, the jurisdiction is marked \"No Change.\" If there is not enough data to be confident of the trend, the jurisdiction is marked \"Sparse.\"\n- Read more about this data set here."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "nwss_wval#15",
      "doc_id": "nwss_wval",
      "heading": "Using the Wastewater Viral Activity Level > Annual recalibration",
      "text": "The annual review process occurs each summer, before the next fall/winter respiratory season, with updated methods published and applied each August. CDC works collaboratively with wastewater and infectious disease experts to evaluate method performance, including comparisons with clinical data from the National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP). When CDC updates WVAL methods, updated methods are applied to all historical data, not just to new data going forward. This means past and current WVALs are updated using the recalibrated approach, ensuring that comparisons across sites and over time reflect differences in wastewater viral activity, not differences in how the data were analyzed."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#17",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Emergency department visit trends",
      "text": "- A Health Service Area (HSA) is a geographic area that combines one or more counties that are next to each other to form a region. The region is relatively self-contained with respect to hospital care.\n- HSAs are used to represent county-level trends in emergency department visits because patients may seek care in their own county or neighboring counties.\n- The National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS), which is part of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), was originally responsible for defining HSAs.\n- More information on HSAs is available here.\n\n- The data available to state and local public health and the timelines for updating data may vary from what is reported to CDC.\n- CDC may use different methods or tools than your state or county and will apply these methods to jurisdictions across the country allowing for more direct comparisons between jurisdictions."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#16",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Emergency department visit trends",
      "text": "- Finally, wastewater testing is done on human waste disposed of through the municipal sewage system. Wastewater data will not reflect populations whose waste is disposed of outside the wastewater system, such as people wearing diapers, or households on septic tanks or otherwise not connected to a municipal sewer system.\n\n- A Health Service Area (HSA) is a geographic area that combines one or more counties that are next to each other to form a region. The region is relatively self-contained with respect to hospital care.\n- HSAs are used to represent county-level trends in emergency department visits because patients may seek care in their own county or neighboring counties.\n- The National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS), which is part of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), was originally responsible for defining HSAs.\n- More information on HSAs is available here."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_sources#5",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_sources",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data sources > 2. Emergency department visits: NSSP",
      "text": "- **Geography:** State, and CDC health service areas (HSAs) within the state. HSAs are identified by their NCI HSA code. An HSA is a multi-county region; every member county carries the same value. The national row is excluded.\n- **Supplementary coverage file:** CDC's NSSP county facility-participation map ([coverage-map.csv](https://www.cdc.gov/nssp/documents/coverage-map.csv)), downloaded on 2026-08-06. It is used only to derive the ED `participation_status` field (see [METHODS.md](METHODS.md)). It is a single point-in-time snapshot, not a weekly history."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "nssp_dataset#0",
      "doc_id": "nssp_dataset",
      "heading": "NSSP Emergency Department Visit Trajectories by State and Sub-State Regions",
      "text": "NSSP Emergency Department (ED) Visit Trajectories by State and Sub-State Regions- COVID-19, Flu, RSV, Combined. This dataset provides the percentage of emergency department patient visits for the specified pathogen of all ED patient visits for the specified geographic part of the country that were observed for the given week from data submitted to the National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP). In addition, the trend over time is characterized as increasing, decreasing or no change, with exceptions for when there are no data available, the data are too sparse, or there are not enough data to compute a trend. These data are to provide awareness of how the weekly trend is changing for the given geographic region.\n\nNote that the reported sub-state trends are from Health Service Areas (HSA) and the data reported from the health care facilities located within the given HSA. Health Service Areas are regions of one or more counties that align to patterns of care seeking. The HSA level data are reported for each county in the HSA.\n\nMore information on HSAs is available here."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_methods#4",
      "doc_id": "oad_methods",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData methods > 2. Geography",
      "text": "- **State:** identified by its two-digit FIPS code.\n- **Health service area (HSA):** the sub-state unit in which CDC publishes NSSP data. It is a multi-county region, and every member county shares one value, so HSA values are not independent county measurements. Some HSAs appear in the source with no ED values at any time.\n- **Wastewater site:** the unit in which CDC publishes wastewater data. A site can serve several counties, and a few serve counties in two states; a site is listed under the state given by the source. Sites are not split, weighted, or reassigned to counties or HSAs in these tables.\n- **Census:** state level only. No official correspondence exists between HSAs or wastewater service areas and Census geographies, so no population weighting to those units is attempted."
    }
  ]
