    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#18",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Display criteria for sub-state levels (categories)",
      "text": "Data representing less than 5% of the population has a hatching, or lined, pattern on the map, indicating the wastewater viral activity level may not show the full picture for that state or territory. States or territories without sufficient data are indicated as \"No data\". This can be because data was not reported from the state or territory, or the monitored sites do not have enough data to calculate the wastewater viral activity level.\n\nWastewater data will not include waste that may be disposed of outside the wastewater system, such as dirty diapers."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#5",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Wastewater viral activity level",
      "text": "- Wastewater testing for influenza displayed in these visuals only detects influenza A viruses and does not distinguish between influenza A subtypes e.g. seasonal influenza A(H1N1), seasonal influenza A(H3N2) and avian influenza A(H5).\n- Wastewater data will not include waste that may be disposed of outside the wastewater system, such as from people wearing diapers. This likely explains why trends in wastewater data for RSV do not always align with emergency department visits for RSV, which causes severe disease primarily in infants under 6 months and older adults.\n- States or territories with limited coverage are those where wastewater monitoring covers a small segment (less than 5%) of the state or territory population. Because data from limited coverage areas may not fully reflect infections for the larger population, wastewater viral activity levels are not shown on the 'Your community snapshot'. Levels are shown in limited coverage areas with hatch marks on the activity level page. These data are also made available on the National Wastewater Surveillance System homepage."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#17",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Display criteria for sub-state levels (categories)",
      "text": "RSV and COVID-19 are limited to nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs), also listed as polymerase chain rection tests (PCR). Participating laboratories report weekly to CDC the total number of COVID-19, flu, and RSV tests performed that week and the number of those tests that were positive.\n\nThe map shows the Wastewater Viral Activity Level across US states and territories. Activity levels indicate how much virus is currently detected in the wastewater \u2013 ranging from very low to very high. Wastewater monitoring can detect viruses spreading from one person to another within a community earlier than clinical testing, and before people who are sick go to their healthcare provider or hospital. It can also detect infections in people without symptoms. For more information, see Data Methods.\n\nData representing less than 5% of the population has a hatching, or lined, pattern on the map, indicating the wastewater viral activity level may not show the full picture for that state or territory. States or territories without sufficient data are indicated as \"No data\". This can be because data was not reported from the state or territory, or the monitored sites do not have enough data to calculate the wastewater viral activity level."
    },
