{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#26",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > What Does This Mean for You",
      "text": "Surveillance systems in the Respiratory Illness Data Channel are pieces of a larger puzzle. These systems tell us how virus activity in changing, but they don\u2019t tell us about the full impact on people and communities. Understanding what information is available to track disease activity gives people a better understanding of their risk for illness. This guidance helps people make informed health decisions to protect themselves from respiratory infections, severe disease, and death."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#23",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > What *Isn't* Reported",
      "text": "We cannot directly count people who are sick but do not have symptoms, people who choose not to seek medical care when they are sick, or people who use at-home tests but fail to report their positive results to their doctor. These groups matter because they can still spread illness to other people.\n\nThere are other ways of tracking virus activity, like the CDC's Wastewater Monitoring Program (NWSS). When someone is sick, they can leave traces of the virus in sewer systems through urine, stool, saliva, and mucus. NWSS provides an indicator of the amount of virus in a community. Think of wastewater testing like checking a community's pulse: it doesn't tell you who is sick, but it signals whether more or less virus is circulating. Wastewater monitoring systems cannot tell us if the detected virus is from humans, animals, or environmental sources--or if cases are diagnosed or severe.\n\nEven for the disease data we do have, reporting may be delayed, making it more difficult to anticipate trends in disease activity. Modeling is a way to use data and math to fill in missing information and make short-term predictions about disease."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#21",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Emergency department visit trends",
      "text": "CDC monitors surveillance data for respiratory illnesses, like COVID-19, influenza, and RSV. Illness surveillance is how health agencies watch for and gather information about sickness so they can spot trends and respond quickly. This information helps people understand the levels of disease in their communities, as well as their risk for infection, severe disease, and death.\n\nPrograms that track respiratory illnesses use patient data reported to state and local health departments and to the CDC. Because of this, reported disease numbers usually show fewer cases than are actually happening because not everyone gets tested or reports results. The below graphic highlights the respiratory illness data we have, what we don't, and how we estimate the missing information."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#22",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > What *Is* Reported",
      "text": "The Respiratory Illness Data Channel shares data collected from health systems when people seek care. The CDC collects data from these systems:\n\n- Positive clinical tests (from NREVSS \u2014 National Respiratory and Enteric Virus Surveillance System)\n- Emergency department visits for respiratory problems (from NSSP \u2014 National Syndromic Surveillance Program)\n- Hospitalizations for respiratory illness (from NHSN, National Hospital Safety Network, and RESPNET, Respiratory Virus Hospitalization Surveillance Network)\n- Deaths caused by respiratory illness (from NVSS \u2014 National Vital Statistics System)\n\nThese systems **only** collect data from people who have sought medical care. The Respiratory Illness Data Channel also shares data from these additional systems to better understand respiratory illness risk in communities:\n\n- Virus concentration in wastewater systems (from NWSS \u2013 National Wastewater Surveillance System)\n- Immunizations to protect against respiratory illness (from NIS \u2013 National Immunization Survey)"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#54",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > August 8, 2025 release",
      "text": "1\\. Launch of New Page - \"Other Respiratory Illnesses Going Around\"\n\n- What:\n  - Added a new page titled \"Other Respiratory Illness Going Around.\"\n  - This page is designed to inform the general public about various respiratory viruses and bacteria beyond COVID-19, flu and RSV.\n  - Included in the new page is a chart of non-pathogen-specific clinical condition trends by age group observed in emergency departments.\n- Why:\n  - Our objective is to provide data for a wider range of respiratory illnesses. In doing so, we strive to elevate community knowledge and insight into the evolving landscape of respiratory illness.\n  - Providing data on clinical conditions helps users understand the impact of respiratory illnesses on different age demographics within emergency departments.\n\n2\\. Design updates for greater usability in February and early March 2025"
    }
  ]
}

