{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#65",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > October 4, 2024 release",
      "text": "  - Emergency department data in 'Your community snapshot' previously only monitored changes in the percent of people going to the emergency department for treatment of COVID-19, flu, and RSV. With the addition of these new categories, you can quickly see current levels of activity alongside whether visits are increasing, decreasing, or not changing. The Activity Levels page will continue to show the weekly percentage of emergency department visits for each virus over time if you want to monitor more specific changes.\n\n4\\. Adding new data to the Respiratory Virus Activity Levels page."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#64",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > October 4, 2024 release",
      "text": "3\\. Enhancing emergency department data.\n\n- What:\n  - We added a way to understand how often COVID, flu, and RSV is being seen in emergency departments in 'Your community snapshot.' Levels are categorized as Minimal, Low, Moderate, High, or Very High.\n  - These categories are relative to a seasonal baseline (periods when circulation of the virus is low) established for each state to better account for differences in emergency department use behaviors across the US.\n- Why:\n  - Levels will help you see how the number of people sick enough they need to go to their local emergency room, either because of the severity of their illness or as a replacement for primary care, compared to the number of visits for those reasons when virus activity was low."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#62",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > October 4, 2024 release",
      "text": "**Major updates:**\n\n1\\. Enhancing 'Your community snapshot\u2019 for greater insight and usability.\n\n- What:\n  - The community snapshot now includes explanatory text for each indicator that outlines \"what it is\" and \"why it matters\" giving users a clearer understanding of the data presented.\n  - Additional links allow users to access detailed data and learn more about each indicator.\n- Why:\n  - These enhancements are designed to provide users with essential context and background.\n  - By expanding the snapshot with descriptive text and further resources, we aim to increase transparency around the data.\n\n2\\. Adding new Acute Respiratory Illness (ARI) metric to capture a broader range of respiratory illnesses requiring medical care."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#63",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > October 4, 2024 release",
      "text": "- What: We utilize the ARI metric to categorize the level of respiratory illnesses as Minimal, Low, Moderate, High, or Very High in two places:\n  - 'Your community snapshot' on the main page of the Respiratory Illness Data Channel alongside other state and community data.\n  - Respiratory Virus Activity Levels, where we replaced the map based on influenza-like illness (ILI) with a map using the new ARI metric to capture the level of respiratory illness activity across the US. ILI data continues to be available on the ILINet State Activity Indicator Map.\n- Why:\n  - The ARI metric provides a broader picture than ILI for evaluating overall respiratory illness trends. Some respiratory illnesses do not have fever as a main symptom and are not captured consistently in measures using ILI, which uses fever as a criterion.\n  - The new summary metric characterizes overall ARI activity to monitor the combined effects of multiple respiratory illnesses and when used in combination with laboratory confirmed pathogen specific surveillance can help monitor trends in illness associated with respiratory pathogens."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#66",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > October 4, 2024 release",
      "text": "4\\. Adding new data to the Respiratory Virus Activity Levels page.\n\n- What:\n  - We are adding epidemic trend information for COVID-19 and flu to the activity levels page. The epidemic trend is measured by the effective reproduction number, or Rt a key metric that indicates whether infections are increasing (*R<sub>t</sub>* > 1), decreasing (*R<sub>t</sub>* \\< 1), or not changing (*R<sub>t</sub>* \u2248 1). These data are calculated and displayed at the state level. Read more here and in the Behind the Model.\n  - *R<sub>t</sub>* estimates are derived from emergency department visit data reported through the National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP) and are updated each week by the Center for Forecasting and Outbreak Analytics. in collaboration with the National Center for Immunizations and Respiratory Diseases.\n- Why:\n  - The interactive maps will allow users to visualize and understand the estimated current trends in COVID-19 and flu transmission across states.\n\n5\\. People most impacted page reorganization and redesign"
    }
  ]
}

