{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_dictionary#19",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_dictionary",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data dictionary > 5. Measures",
      "text": "| `feature_name` | Source | Pathogens | Unit | `definition_status` | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `ed_visit_percent` | NSSP | covid, influenza, rsv, combined | `percent_ed_visits` | confirmed | Percent of all ED visits in the region and week that match the pathogen's syndrome definition. |\n| `ed_visit_percent_smoothed` | NSSP | covid, influenza, rsv, combined | `percent_ed_visits` | confirmed | CDC's smoothed version of the same percent. |\n| `ed_trend_category` | NSSP | covid, influenza, rsv | `categorical_trend` | uncertain | CDC's trend label: `Increasing`, `Decreasing`, `No Change`, or a data-availability note: `Data Unavailable`, `Limited Data`, `Sparse`, `No Data`. |"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_dictionary#9",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_dictionary",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data dictionary > 2. Long tables",
      "text": "| Column | Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `feature_unit` | text | `count`, `percent_ed_visits`, `wval_index_not_cross_pathogen_comparable`, `ordinal_category`, `categorical_trend`, or `categorical_coverage_status`. |\n| `observation_state` | text | Reliability flag for this value; see [section 6](#6-reliability-flags). |\n| `definition_status` | text | `confirmed`: the measure is fully defined by the source documentation. `observed_definition_uncertain`: usable, but either the source documentation leaves part of the definition open or the field is derived by this dataset. See section 5 for which measures are which. |\n\n**Unique key:**"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_dictionary#14",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_dictionary",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data dictionary > 3. Single-source wide tables",
      "text": "| Column name | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `<measure>_<pathogen>` | the value (`weekly_feature` in the long table) |\n| `<measure>_<pathogen>_flag` | its reliability flag (`observation_state` in the long table) |\n\nExamples: `hospital_admission_count_covid` and `hospital_admission_count_covid_flag`, `ed_visit_percent_smoothed_rsv`, `wastewater_activity_category_influenza`. The ED `participation_status` has no pathogen dimension, so its columns are `participation_status_not_applicable` and `participation_status_not_applicable_flag`.\n\nRows exist only for (region, week) combinations the source reports. Within a row, a blank value **with** a flag is a reported gap (for example `incidental_missing`). A blank value **and** a blank flag means the source has no record for that pathogen in that region and week."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "mmwr_ww_ed_wi#9",
      "doc_id": "mmwr_ww_ed_wi",
      "heading": "MMWR: Wastewater as a complement to ED visit data, influenza A and RSV, Wisconsin 2022\u201323 > Methods > Statistical Analyses",
      "text": "Data on wastewater surveillance viral concentrations and ED visits were paired by surveillance week, and Kendall\u2019s tau (a nonparametric measure of correlation) was used to assess statistical agreement between the surveillance systems. Kendall\u2019s tau, which has been applied in previous wastewater surveillance literature, was used to quantify the relationship between the two data sources; higher values indicate a stronger correlation. R software (version 4.1.3; R Foundation) was used to conduct all statistical analyses. This activity was reviewed by CDC and conducted consistent with applicable federal law and CDC policy.<sup>\u00b6</sup>"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#10",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Methodology for using acute respiratory illness (ARI) as a surveillance metric to describe levels (categories) of emergency department (ED) visits:",
      "text": "1. First, we calculated the weekly percentage of ED visits attributed to each pathogen and ARI overall within the identified lowest activity periods.\n2. Next, we calculated the mean, M, and standard deviation, S, attributed to each pathogen and ARI overall within the identified lowest activity periods. The mean was considered the baseline value for each state and was used as the top value for the Very Low category.\n3. Then, we computed the maximum number of standard deviations above the mean for any observed week over all weeks in the three previous seasons (N = (maximum number of standard deviations - M) / S).\n4. The maximum number of standard deviations (N) was divided by 4 and multiplied by the standard deviation (S = standard deviation). This value was denoted as D, so D = (N / 4) \\* S."
    }
  ]
}

