{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#60",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Release Notes > December 20, 2024 release",
      "text": "2\\. Updated the data notes for the Respiratory Virus Activity Level page\n\n- What:\n  - We added more details to the methods used for the Acute Respiratory Index baseline metric for measuring the level of respiratory illness activity.\n- Why:\n  - These changes will help others understand the methods and allow them to replicate the approach.\n\n3\\. Design updates for greater usability in November & December 2024"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#10",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Methodology for using acute respiratory illness (ARI) as a surveillance metric to describe levels (categories) of emergency department (ED) visits:",
      "text": "1. First, we calculated the weekly percentage of ED visits attributed to each pathogen and ARI overall within the identified lowest activity periods.\n2. Next, we calculated the mean, M, and standard deviation, S, attributed to each pathogen and ARI overall within the identified lowest activity periods. The mean was considered the baseline value for each state and was used as the top value for the Very Low category.\n3. Then, we computed the maximum number of standard deviations above the mean for any observed week over all weeks in the three previous seasons (N = (maximum number of standard deviations - M) / S).\n4. The maximum number of standard deviations (N) was divided by 4 and multiplied by the standard deviation (S = standard deviation). This value was denoted as D, so D = (N / 4) \\* S."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#11",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Methodology for using acute respiratory illness (ARI) as a surveillance metric to describe levels (categories) of emergency department (ED) visits:",
      "text": "- Using X = percent of ED visits in a week for each pathogen and ARI overall, we classified activity levels as follows:\n  - Very Low: X \\<= M, activity at or below baseline\n  - Low: M \\< X \\<= M + D\n  - Moderate: M + D \\< X \\<= M + 2D\n  - High: M + 2D \\< X \\<= + 3D\n  - Very High: M + 3D \\< X\n- **Missouri:** Due to data quality issues, Missouri's historical data before MMWR Week 10 of 2025 (March 2-8, 2025) could not be used for computing levels, thus leaving insufficient data. To address this, data from the rest of HHS Region 7 (Iowa, Kansas, and Nebraska) were used in place of Missouri's own before Week 10 of 2025 to compute the 2026-2027 levels for Missouri; for Week 10 of 2025 and later in the historical baseline years, Missouri's data was used."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#2",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Overall respiratory illness activity",
      "text": "Explanation:\n\n- Respiratory illness activity is monitored using the acute respiratory illness (ARI) definition. ARI captures a broad range of emergency department visits for respiratory illnesses, ranging from the common cold to potentially severe illnesses like flu (influenza), RSV and COVID-19. It captures a broader range of respiratory illnesses, including those that do not present with fever, compared to the previously shown influenza-like illness (ILI) metric (Note: the ILI metric is still being used for flu monitoring).\n- Respiratory illness activity levels are based on comparing the percent of emergency department visits for ARI during the current week to the average percent of visits during times when COVID-19, flu and RSV visits were low (Note: other respiratory illness-related visits may still be occurring). These low points are considered \"baseline\" levels of respiratory illness activity The activity level is based on how elevated the current week is from the \"baseline\" period. See Activity Levels data notes for the detailed methodology.\n- Emergency department data are collected through the National Syndromic Surveillance Program."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#7",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Methodology for using acute respiratory illness (ARI) as a surveillance metric to describe levels (categories) of emergency department (ED) visits:",
      "text": "Acute respiratory illness (ARI) is a non-specific syndrome that captures a broad range of diagnoses from emergency department visits for respiratory illnesses, ranging from the common cold to infections that can be severe including COVID-19, influenza, and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV). An ARI-related metric classifies the percentage of ED visits due to ARI into 5 levels (categories): Very Low, Low, Moderate, High, and Very High at the regional, state, and local levels."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_methods#4",
      "doc_id": "oad_methods",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData methods > 2. Geography",
      "text": "- **State:** identified by its two-digit FIPS code.\n- **Health service area (HSA):** the sub-state unit in which CDC publishes NSSP data. It is a multi-county region, and every member county shares one value, so HSA values are not independent county measurements. Some HSAs appear in the source with no ED values at any time.\n- **Wastewater site:** the unit in which CDC publishes wastewater data. A site can serve several counties, and a few serve counties in two states; a site is listed under the state given by the source. Sites are not split, weighted, or reassigned to counties or HSAs in these tables.\n- **Census:** state level only. No official correspondence exists between HSAs or wastewater service areas and Census geographies, so no population weighting to those units is attempted."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_sources#8",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_sources",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data sources > 4. Census context",
      "text": "- **Datasets:**\n  - American Community Survey (ACS) 5-year estimates, 2022 vintage. Data Profile and Subject Table variables, retrieved from the [Census Data API](https://www.census.gov/data/developers/data-sets/acs-5year.html).\n  - Population Estimates Program, 2022 state totals ([NST-EST2022-ALLDATA](https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2022/state/totals/NST-EST2022-ALLDATA.csv)).\n- **Publisher:** US Census Bureau.\n- **Geography:** State only. No sub-state Census join is made: HSAs have no official Census correspondence, and wastewater service areas are not Census geographies.\n- **Variables:** 23 ACS variables (estimates and margins of error), listed with their ACS codes in [DATA_DICTIONARY.md](DATA_DICTIONARY.md#census-variables), plus `POPESTIMATE2022`."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_sources#0",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_sources",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data sources",
      "text": "All inputs are public data published by the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and the US Census Bureau. They were downloaded as the full nationwide files; each state's tables are a filter of those files, not separately obtained data. Every release used is kept unmodified in the public [archive of CDC releases](https://github.com/jiandewu/ObsAwareData-pipeline), with its release date and SHA-256 checksum."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_methods#3",
      "doc_id": "oad_methods",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData methods > 1. Processing overview",
      "text": "   - `<st>_nhsn_wide.csv`, `<st>_nssp_wide.csv`, `<st>_nwss_wide.csv`: one row per region and week.\n   - `<st>_all_sources_wide.csv`: one row per week. The state-level NHSN and NSSP columns, each HSA's NSSP columns, each wastewater site's columns, and the state Census variables are placed side by side.\n   - No values are aggregated, averaged, interpolated, or imputed.\n7. **Join Census context** at the state level only.\n8. **Check and package.** Each state's quality report confirms:\n   - Unique keys\n   - Cell-by-cell agreement between every wide table and the long table\n   - A complete weekly sequence\n   - Agreement between the latest view and the revision history"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_readme#1",
      "doc_id": "oad_readme",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData README",
      "text": "| Source | What it measures | Geography | Weeks covered |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NHSN Hospital Respiratory Data | New hospital admissions and number of reporting hospitals | State | <!-- auto:weeks_nhsn -->2020-08-08 to 2026-10-10<!-- /auto:weeks_nhsn --> |\n| NSSP Emergency Department Visit Trajectories | Percent of ED visits for each pathogen, CDC trend label | State and CDC health service areas (multi-county regions) | <!-- auto:weeks_nssp -->2022-10-01 to 2026-10-10<!-- /auto:weeks_nssp --> |\n| NWSS Wastewater Viral Activity Level | Wastewater viral activity index and category | Individual wastewater sampling sites | <!-- auto:weeks_nwss -->2022-01-01 to 2026-10-10<!-- /auto:weeks_nwss --> |\n| Census ACS 5-year 2022 and Population Estimates 2022 | Population and socioeconomic context | State | Static |"
    }
