  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#11",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Methodology for using acute respiratory illness (ARI) as a surveillance metric to describe levels (categories) of emergency department (ED) visits:",
      "text": "- Using X = percent of ED visits in a week for each pathogen and ARI overall, we classified activity levels as follows:\n  - Very Low: X \\<= M, activity at or below baseline\n  - Low: M \\< X \\<= M + D\n  - Moderate: M + D \\< X \\<= M + 2D\n  - High: M + 2D \\< X \\<= + 3D\n  - Very High: M + 3D \\< X\n- **Missouri:** Due to data quality issues, Missouri's historical data before MMWR Week 10 of 2025 (March 2-8, 2025) could not be used for computing levels, thus leaving insufficient data. To address this, data from the rest of HHS Region 7 (Iowa, Kansas, and Nebraska) were used in place of Missouri's own before Week 10 of 2025 to compute the 2026-2027 levels for Missouri; for Week 10 of 2025 and later in the historical baseline years, Missouri's data was used."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#15",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Display criteria for sub-state levels (categories)",
      "text": "- To assess if an HSA meets the criteria for classification based on sufficient data, data weeks from Week 1 to Week 26 of 2026 were considered. If the total number of ED visits in an HSA during these weeks was equal to or greater than the corresponding V value, it indicated sufficient data. However, if the total number of weeks meeting this minimum count fell below 80% (21 out of 26 weeks), the level (category) was not displayed for that HSA in any week. The percentage of ED visits for COVID-19, influenza, or RSV may still be displayed even if the level (category) is not displayed.\n\n- **Sources**: COVID-19 and RSV: National Respiratory and Enteric Virus Surveillance System (NREVSS), a sentinel network of laboratories located through the US, includes clinical, public health and commercial laboratories. Flu: Clinical laboratory test results from NREVSS and flu collaborating laboratories; more details about influenza virologic surveillance are available."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_readme#1",
      "doc_id": "oad_readme",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData README",
      "text": "| Source | What it measures | Geography | Weeks covered |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NHSN Hospital Respiratory Data | New hospital admissions and number of reporting hospitals | State | <!-- auto:weeks_nhsn -->2020-08-08 to 2026-10-10<!-- /auto:weeks_nhsn --> |\n| NSSP Emergency Department Visit Trajectories | Percent of ED visits for each pathogen, CDC trend label | State and CDC health service areas (multi-county regions) | <!-- auto:weeks_nssp -->2022-10-01 to 2026-10-10<!-- /auto:weeks_nssp --> |\n| NWSS Wastewater Viral Activity Level | Wastewater viral activity index and category | Individual wastewater sampling sites | <!-- auto:weeks_nwss -->2022-01-01 to 2026-10-10<!-- /auto:weeks_nwss --> |\n| Census ACS 5-year 2022 and Population Estimates 2022 | Population and socioeconomic context | State | Static |"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_dictionary#15",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_dictionary",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data dictionary > 4. All-sources wide table",
      "text": "`<st>_all_sources_wide.csv` has one row per week, spanning every week in any source (<!-- auto:weeks_all_sources -->2020-08-08 to 2026-10-10<!-- /auto:weeks_all_sources -->), and one column per region, site, measure, and pathogen. Nothing is aggregated or averaged: each ED region and each wastewater site keeps its own columns. Column counts range from about 100 to 3,200 depending on how many regions and sites a state has.\n\n**Column-naming rule:** `[<prefix>]<measure>_<pathogen>[_flag]`. The prefix identifies where the value comes from:"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_dictionary#17",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_dictionary",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data dictionary > 4. All-sources wide table",
      "text": "The measure names never overlap between NHSN and NSSP, so the unprefixed state-level columns are unambiguous. Every value column except `census_*` has a matching `_flag` column.\n\nBlank cells:\n\n- A blank flag means the source has no record for that region and week, for example weeks before a site started reporting, or weeks after a source's last published week. <!-- auto:source_end_dates -->All three sources end on 2026-10-10.<!-- /auto:source_end_dates -->\n- A blank value with a non-blank flag is a reported gap.\n\nEvery wastewater site in the state appears here, including the few that serve counties in more than one ED region or more than one state.\n\n`observation_week` is the first column. To map an `HSA<code>` back to its name and member counties, look up that `analysis_region_id` in `<st>_nssp_wide.csv`."
